    "turn_text": "Well, Sir, well, thank you for having me. So I'm a fifth generation farm kid from rural Kansas. My dad became a police officer. We moved off the family farm when I was young, but we continued to work on those family farms. My American dream was to become a physician. And though there were people said, oh, you can't do that because no one from your family's ever went to college. So how in the world do you think you can get all the way through med school? And I would sit there and say, well, it's because I work hard. And by the way, my grades are really, really, really good. And I love math. I love chemistry. I was the kid that would read the encyclopedia for fun. I read the newspapers just because I thought that's what you were supposed to do. I mean, since I was little, little. So I got to live out that dream. Thanks to loving parents, great teachers, just a supporting family with to rural Kansas in the middle of about 60,000, 70,000 people. I was the only OBGYN there. I took lasers there, laparoscopes, infertility treatments as well. and delivered a baby a day on average for over 25 years. Took a group of doctors and we built our own hospital. It was part of a community bank still remained in agriculture. I have a little entrepreneurial flair, a little bit of investment in oil and gas industry. When you're a doctor in a small town, you get all these",
