  {
    "episode_id": 96850,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96850.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 15,
    "turn_text": "the big picture is the first two years were extraordinarily fun. We have a control of the House, the Senate, and the White House. And we were making changes. We were working with the White House. We were getting things done like expanding healthcare associations and transparency for hospitals, all sorts of neat things that I came here to help fix. Well, then that came to a screeching halt in 2018. And that's really still where we are. Couldn't be more frustrating right now. I'm the person that's looking 10 years ahead. And so if you're a very proactive, so instead we've been in this defensive posture. And now to back up a little bit more into the political side of it. When I was running for Congress, people would ask me those questions, well, what are your politics? And I would say, well, I'm an American. I'm a Kansan. I'm a father. I teach Sunday school. I coach sports. And sure, I identify with conservative principles, especially the values part of this. I believe in the sanctity of life delivered 5,000 babies. I believe life begins at conception. I believe in in balancing our checkbook every month. I don't like debt. I'm out of personally and have no debt in my life. So I'll bring those same financial wisdom, this business background and figure out where we're going. And it's interesting that I'm going to back up here and say, who are your favorite presidents? And when I identified with favorite presidents, it wasn't based on, I didn't know, when you're eight or 12 years old, you don't know, they were Republican, they're Democrat, what their policies are. And I said there and said, well, I identify with the course, President Eisenhower from Kansas, Teddy Roosevelt. We've got him right here. There's Teddy and I kind of like Thomas Jefferson. I love his, his, his, science mind, you know, different things like that. And then here I am in the Senate now and I've developed this, what I would call populist brand, probably for maybe one of the most conservative folks up here, what you would think is as traditionally conservative, but I had this populist streak in me and even maybe a little bit of a libertarian streak that really folks back home and be very happy just to be left alone by the federal government. So bringing all those tools together, as you said, this new maybe going back to our roots Republican Party of less government protector constitutional rights and just get the this government leave us the heck alone.",
