  {
    "episode_id": 96850,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96850.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 17,
    "turn_text": "It feels really good. If folks can go back and look at our press conference we did earlier this week, standing beside Marcia Blackburn, Ted Cruz, Mike Lee, John Kennedy, Ron John, Mike Lee, and more. But this group of folks that my friends back home calls the Patriots. that we are the modern day patriots. And there's probably about 18 of us that work on something we call the steering committee. Mike Ali has been the chairman of that committee for quite some time, really trying to focus on the true constitution and bringing conservative principles. But still, I think what's moved the wind right now is just the critical mass. that if you look at the last group of, I don't know, eight or 12 senators elected, all of us kind of have this popular streak. I don't think it's the driving factor. I still went more interested in balancing the budget than anything else. I think that would be the biggest impact on my grandchildren's generation. But it is really interesting. I think we've reached a critical mass. And we could see four or five or six more of this populist brand senators come next year. And that's going to influence, you know, who elect the leader to be at the center as well. But I think that we're representing, this is what we're hearing from folks back home is they, the Republican party is becoming the party of folks that carry their lunch pedal to work and of small businesses, as opposed to being ruled by big banks and big corporations.",
