    "turn_idx": 24,
    "turn_text": "So I recall a Saturday afternoon. I'm pretty sure it was a Saturday. And why we were here, I don't remember why. And they finally kind of presented to the deal to us what it looked like. And I think, including myself, we just lost it. We get together every Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday at lunch, about 40 of the 50 members of the Senate show up. We have lunch together. These are where the heated discussions happen. Just like if you ever had a family discussion, family business or, you know, what to do with the farm. This is one of those discussions. We lost it. And you could just see the room temperature change where our leadership felt like that they were out on a plank and go in the wrong direction. And basically, leadership walked out and did a little gaggle with the press and said, we're pulling the plug on this thing. We don't support it. It was certainly maybe the defining moment of when this tide changed, where our caucus changed to say, wait a second, there's a new sheriff in town. And not that I'm that chair, but we collectively are driving a lot more of the policy right now, I think. We failed a couple of times. We're winning more than we're losing now, though.",
