    "turn_text": "going. Right. So you're going to take 60 votes on the Senate to do most anything. 60. And as long as our caucus sticks together, all 49 of us, we can pretty much freeze the Senate Democrats out of anything. It takes 60, not 50 plus one. So they have to get some of us to flip. And you all the house side, they had this, they try not to do anything less. They have the majority of the majority, but our leadership continues to walk into these buzz saws without the majority of the majority of the Republicans. And the spending bills are great examples of this. is that those spending bills do not represent the majority of the majority, and we begged our caucus, Republican caucus, to say strong, don't give them one vote until we, in my case, wanted to balance the budget and fund Israel. Four times I've taken standalone funding for Israel to the floor, and each time the Democrats shoot it down. So we have that power in the Senate much more than an individual person does on the house side and the other piece and this is really inside baseball. The Senate runs in what's called unanimous",
