  {
    "episode_id": 96850,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96850.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 36,
    "turn_text": "So there's actually been 21 impeachments total on the house side that have been successful. Four of those, the person died, they resigned, whatever the case was. But the other 17 were all prosecuted. They all had an impeachment trial. So what's supposed to happen is we're supposed to be sworn in as jurors and judge really in this case the senators are and have a trial. I think people probably recall what happened with President Trump that there were two impeachment trials. the Republicans controlled the Senate with the first one. They could have tabled it. Right. But that would be what we would call a nuclear option. We think if Schumer tables this, I would consider that a nuclear option and any type of nuclear retaliation as far as I'm concerned is on the table. And I would take us back to 2013 when Harry Reid was this Democrat Senate majority leader and he changed the the bar for how many votes it would take to become a judge. He lowered it from 60 down to the 50. And at that point in time, it changed history, it changed precedent. We call that the nuclear option. Sure enough, and whenever, when Gorsuch, 2017, I believe, when Gorsuch is the Supreme Court nominee, it come back to bite him in the butt. Because we only had the 50 rather than the 60 of us. We would have never got to 60 on Neil Gorsuch or the rest of them. So we think that this is a big and nuclear bomb as what Harry Reid did back in 2013.",
