    "turn_idx": 70,
    "turn_text": "So certainly it's one of the reasons I came to Congress was to fix health care and more specifically the cost of health care. You know, this affordable care act, what? So the government is supplementing each ACA policy on the average of $20,000 per year now. It's $20,000 per year. And then the customer, the patient back home, they're paying $300 a month. And they're thinking it's relatively free, but it's not. It's costing American taxpayers $20,000. And it is a bigger deal, this regulation part of it though, that the way you drive the cost down of healthcare is anything that makes healthcare more transparent. anything that drives innovation, anything that allows the patient to become consumers again. You know, right now when a patient would come to me and we say, look, you need to have surgery, the first question is, does Medicare cover this? Does insurance cover it? Well, then it's fine. So somehow we need to make them consumers again. I'm going to back up from that though and say, we need to work on nutrition. We really do. Food is medicine. If you want me to save Medicare, I need Americans to eat a little healthier and to walk a little bit more. I'm not saying you got to go to the gym. I'd be",
