    "turn_text": "them. The nutrition and exercise one is particularly interesting to me because I think in a funny way, as the Republican parties political perspective on big businesses changed over the last 15 years, that has actually had implications for its view on that issue. Whereas maybe 15 years ago, if you said what you said, people would say, hands off my Frito-Lay, now people are saying, well, maybe the biggest food companies in the world don't have my best interests at heart. And so I think that, and I've seen this firsthand, but I think the, you know, our voters are much more primed to hear a different message on this than they would have been historically. And I know definitely, you know, kind of on the leading edge of right wing subcultures on the internet, people are open to all sorts of crazy ideas. They're talking about raw milk. They're talking about, you know, eating eggs and carnivore diets and so on and so forth. Would just be really curious to have you dive a little bit more into that. I mean, it feels like there's just, there's so many barriers in front of Americans eating healthier. I mean, at a certain level, right? I mean, the tastiest food, the food engineered to be hyper-palatable is this highly processed food that's making us sick and fat. What should we do about that?",
