  {
    "episode_id": 96850,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96850.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 86,
    "turn_text": "So all those types of things. So one of the things I've tried to do up here on ag policy, 85% of a farm bill is actually food programs. is try to reward people. Rather than trying to be a food manager, the food police is to try to say, we're going to give you double the value. If you got a food stamp here that's worth a dollar, if you buy these particular fresh fruits and vegetables, then we're going to give you double the value and reward those because they are expensive. So reward the value for that, try to teach people new habits, try to emphasize working with our food banks. Look, three day old donuts are really cheap. But instead trying to get healthy foods in there, another thing we're trying to work on is access to primary care. So look, I would give to you that if you break your arm, if you have a heart attack, you go to ER, regardless of your ability to pay, they're going to take care of you, but not everybody has access to primary care. Meaningful affordable access to primary care. So something I'm working on with Bernie Sanders is increased funding for these community health centers. And one of the things we would do is bring nutrition into that clinic. And I don't think you need a PhD nutrition. The best coaches I've ever seen, whether it was nutrition or taking a new medicine or nurses.",
