  {
    "episode_id": 96850,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96850.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 95,
    "turn_text": "All these personal experiences, yeah. Well, it's interesting because the primary care thing goes to some of the issues that are emergent in the medical profession. I know that the most salient issue that people talk about is the absence of doctors in rural areas. It seems to me that the chain of causality here is that medical school has gotten more and more expensive. It's also become more and more of sort of a status profession. And so people are attracted to super specialties and big cities. There's not a lot of primary care physicians going out to rural Kansas, going out to the communities that just need a basic country doctor. How do we fix that?",
