    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Assert / Elaborate"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 96850,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96850.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 96,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, exactly. So this is certainly an issue that I've dealt with for 30 years. This has been a problem in Kansas since before I went to medical schools, getting doctors to go to rural America. And there's not one simple solution. And they're just part of this big move from rural America to urban America. The big issue back in the small town is the call schedule. I You know, I think that they're trying to figure out ways to bring in doctors to give that one doctor or two doctors in the town relief. That's one of the solutions out there. We're payment of scholarships. If you go to rural America, that's again, something Senator Sanders and I have tried to. And Dr. Cassidy is my ranking member, Bill Cassidy, Senator. He's also, you know, working on these projects as well. Those are some small things that are happening. I think telemedicine is part of the solution. There's going to be more nurse practitioners and PAs out doing more and more things in rural America, but setting up these community health centers. There's now 33 of those across the state of Kansas and half of them are in rural America and trying to bring residents. So as you go through a residency program, this is what happens. You're from, you know, I grew up in a little city called El Dorado, Kansas. You go off to medical school. You meet the girl of your dreams or the guy of your dreams, you know, if you're a woman going to medical school and they're from the big city. they're not going to go back home. So we're trying to get more residencies in primary care and a little bit more of a smaller city where maybe they'll meet each other and say, hey, this is where we want to raise our kids. And that would mean my wife's and my story. We did residency, of course, in Kansas City and Wichita. We went off to Tampa, St. Pete for residency. We had two kids by then. And we said, my daughter's in kindergarten. We didn't like the experience there. Let's go back home. It's time to go home and raise our kids where we know how to",
