    "turn_text": "companies. Look, Americans are paying seven times more every time they use their credit card. It's called a swipe fee, seven times more than what the European Union is, five times more than the Chinese. Even American credit cards in China, Americans are spending five times more and three times more than what our Canadian friends do up north as well. So once again, we have a duopoly, two companies control 85% of the business. This, by the way, regulations. Regulations is what drives consolidation of the industry. So there's no competition happening right there. So, when Dick Durban, hardcore Democrat came to me with a, you know, this, how could we fix this? He wanted a set of fee on it. And I said, no, let's have increased competition. So, when the bank issues a credit card, they can choose Visa or MasterCard to be the network they would run that on. But the bank has to choose a second option. And that's going to help drive the price down. In my opinion, it's going to drive the price down. The folks back home, Main Street, convenience stores, grocery stores, mom and pop stores, they're spending more on swipe fees than they do on their utility bill. More than the one health care for their employees. More than on their rent. This is the number two thing after the cost of labor that's an expense for them. They live in a very competitive industry. You notice the price of gasoline is used within about two cents of each other. They operate at cost plus about one half percent. So I think that this is a situation I'm going to be fighting for Main Street, over Wall Street, you're right, the big banks, credit card companies have spent, I think over $200 million attacking us",
