    "turn_idx": 123,
    "turn_text": "But if I was going to be kept up by something that would be, we're going to spend a trillion dollars on interest this year. I think I see national security a little bit different than a lot of people do up here in the Republican Party. I'm not a warmonger. I just did a trip to Indochina. We have 130,000 military personnel there. We have 100,000 in Europe. China is absolutely a threat to us, but we have an incredible military presence there. And the good thing, good news is Japan's working with us. We've got some really good allies over there now. Australia, India are working with us to put the pressure on them. I think that China has peaked economically, but we underestimate, we mentioned this earlier, we underestimate the cyber security attacks. We underestimate the propaganda. We underestimate the intellectual property theft. China makes 90% of the the fake stuff that comes across our border, whether it's fake watches or fake Nike shoes, whatever it is. So I think that just putting the pressure on them and really just being dependent upon them financially, it's a $300 billion of trade between the two countries, between China and the US. So I get that. But we need to be looking for further newer partners. We wrote this new USMCA trade agreement under President Trump. Mexico was replaced. China is our top export partner in many things, but especially agriculture. Of course, from Kansas, I'm focused on the agriculture part of this. It's not sexy, but it's certainly a national security issue still locked around the after on the axle over COVID-19. It's origin from From China, I was the first person in Congress to come out and say that in January of 2020 and continue to have a body of evidence that shows that. Meanwhile, there's still more and more going on. America is still financing this gain of function research. I mean, my gosh, how much of a slap in the face do we need to say, look, this is more dangerous than nuclear warfare. So there's a couple, couple",
